      "writtenOn": "2026-09-03T09:22:01Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "This Patch \nhttps://gerrit.openbmc.org/c/openbmc/phosphor-logging/+/93483 \nmakes Cereal persistence optional (cereal-persist\u003dfalse). With it disabled, errors/ stays empty, so this fallback logic has nothing to fall back to if the JSON gets corrupted — the entry is just dropped. What\u0027s the intended behavior here once cereal-persist\u003dfalse?",
      "revId": "42b1425c8e6755f8d365cc44bc1f31dc60d5e071",
      "serverId": "adbd0a64-1f21-4d83-b585-671fe73cb6e4"
    },
    {
      "unresolved": true,
      "key": {
        "uuid": "0cd7b1f6_01ac33d8",
        "filename": "/COMMIT_MSG",
        "patchSetId": 10
      },
      "lineNbr": 20,
      "author": {
        "id": 1002753
      },
      "writtenOn": "2026-09-08T02:40:03Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "Intended behaviour with cereal-persist\u003dfalse: \nThe entry is dropped and the failure is logged. That is deliberate - with no second copy there is nothing to recover from, and dropping one unreadable entry is the correct outcome.\n \nConcretely, restore() now records both paths per ID:\n    files[idNum].json   \u003d ...   // scanned from errors-json/\n    files[idNum].cereal \u003d ...   // scanned from errors/\n \nWith cereal-persist\u003dfalse, errors/ has no file for newly created entries, so candidates.cereal stays empty and deserialize() is never reached:\n    else if (!candidates.cereal.empty() \u0026\u0026\n             tryRestore(e, idNum, candidates.cereal, false))\n \nThe fallback is therefore a no-op if the JSON gets corrupted in that configuration and the entry is skipped after logging:\n    Failed to deserialize error entry \u003cid\u003e from \u003cpath\u003e\n \nThe recovery half of this patch is opportunistic.\n  - A zero-filled Cereal file deserializes WITHOUT throwing. A zeroed class version selects the oldest load() branch, so every field reads back as 0 and the entry ends up with id \u003d\u003d 0.\n  - serializeJSON() then runs BEFORE the sanity check and writes errors-json/0.json - a syntactically valid file that no in-memory entry owns, so eraseAll() never deletes it.\n  - The next boot restores it as an Emergency severity entry with a 1970 timestamp on D-Bus.\n \nVerified on AST2700, /var/lib on UBIFS with both settings:\n\nJSON zero-filled, Cereal intact\ncereal-persist\u003dtrue : recovered\ncereal-persist\u003dfalse: recovered (legacy files)\n\nJSON zero-filled, Cereal absent\ncereal-persist\u003dtrue : dropped + logged\ncereal-persist\u003dfalse: dropped + logged\n\nCereal zero-filled, JSON absent\ncereal-persist\u003dtrue : skipped, no 0.json\ncereal-persist\u003dfalse: skipped, no 0.json",
      "parentUuid": "62177816_e2636aad",
      "revId": "42b1425c8e6755f8d365cc44bc1f31dc60d5e071",
      "serverId": "adbd0a64-1f21-4d83-b585-671fe73cb6e4"
    },
