      "writtenOn": "2026-09-10T05:57:32Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "Hi Matt, I am seeing similar symptom on SanMiguel.\nIt looks like JSON file is not exist and cereal file also not completed.\n```\n    \"841\": {\n        \"additional_data\": {},\n        \"event_id\": \"\",\n        \"message\": \"\",\n        \"resolution\": \"\",\n        \"resolved\": false,\n        \"severity\": \"xyz.openbmc_project.Logging.Entry.Level.Emergency\",\n        \"timestamp\": \"1970-01-01T00:00:00.000000000Z\",\n        \"updated_timestamp\": \"1970-01-01T00:00:00.000000000Z\"\n    },\n```\n\nIn current Manager::createEntry(), it serialize the cereal file first, then serialize the JSON file.\n\n1. Power drop in the middle of serializeJSON()\n  - cereal file exist and complete.\n  - JSON file exist but not complete.\n\nIn this case, phosphor-logging service will only take JSON file, but throw it away due to deserializeJSON() failed. so it won\u0027t have a chance to get log from cereal file\n\n\n2. Power drop in the middle of serialize()\n  - cereal file exist but not complete.\n  - JSON file not exist.\n\nIn this case, the service will take cereal file and create a unexpected event entry as above.",
      "parentUuid": "0a96419d_fdea5c28",
      "revId": "42b1425c8e6755f8d365cc44bc1f31dc60d5e071",
      "serverId": "adbd0a64-1f21-4d83-b585-671fe73cb6e4"
    },
